  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Friday...\n\nZonal to weak cyclonic flow was found over the region today as an\nupper low approached the Pac NW, with shortwave ridging ahead of \nthe low over eastern WA/northern ID. Energy moving from west to\neast through the area was producing a few light showers, mainly\nover north and northeastern areas. Expect the showers to gradually\nshift to the north and east through the afternoon. Easterly \nupslope flow should allow stratus to fill back into the foothills \nthis evening, with patchy fog possible in the upslope area \novernight.\n\nAs the low tracks through the Pac NW, then north and east,\nclipping NW MT, this will allow ridging to build over the area on\nSunday bringing a warmer and drier day. Breezy pre-frontal southwest\nwinds will work into the southwest mountains bringing elevated\nfire weather conditions. Breezy southeast winds of 20 to 40 mph \ncan be expected over far southeast MT with lighter easterly winds\nelsewhere. Heading into Sunday night, a cold front associated \nwith the low is progged to drop through the area bringing showers,\nand a few thunderstorms to southeast areas. Snow showers are\npossible over the highest elevations, though little in the way of\naccumulation is expected. PWATs approaching an inch or more and a\nvery narrow corridor of elevated instability may allow for a \nstrong storm or two over the far southeast. \n\nBehind the front, Monday will feature dry, cooler and breezy\nconditions. Widespread westerly wind gusts of 30 to 50 mph can be\nexpected, with the greatest wind gusts along the western \nfoothills. Locations from Big Timber north to Judith Gap have \nroughly a 40-60% chance at seeing wind gusts of 50 mph or greater.\nWill continue to monitor, but have held off on any wind highlites\nat this time as 700 mb winds reach 40 kts. As winds drop and \nskies clear out Monday night, frost is possible (20-40% chance) \nfor some of the colder valley locations.\n\nNW to cyclonic flow behind the departing low will keep temps a \nlittle cooler than normal through midweek. Another shortwave \ndropping out of western Canada brings a low chance (20% or less) \nof light precip Tuesday night and Wednesday, mainly for the\nperiphery of the area, though there is uncertainty with the track\nof the system. Yet another wave lifting in from the west and\nsouthwest could bring additional chances for precipitation for the\nThursday/Friday timeframe (20-40% chance).\n\nHighs are forecast to range from the 70s to lower 80s Sunday,\ndropping into the 60s to lower 70s through Wednesday. Temperatures\nin the upper 60s to 70s are progged for Thursday and Friday. Low\ntemperatures in the 40s to lower 50s can be expected most nights,\nwith some locations Monday night dropping into the 30s. STP",
